  "Number": 3,
  "Question": "For how many ordered pairs of positive integers \\((x, y)\\) is \\(x + 2y = 100?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(33\\)",
    "B": "\\(49\\)",
    "C": "\\(50\\)",
    "D": "\\(99\\)",
    "E": "\\(100\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Once \\(y\\) is chosen, the equation determines \\(x\\) uniquely:\n\\[\nx=100-2y.\n\\]\nBecause both integers must be positive, \\(y\\ge1\\) and \\(100-2y>0\\), giving \\(y<50\\). Thus the possible integer values are exactly\n\\[\ny=1,2,\\ldots,49.\n\\]\nEach gives a positive integer \\(x\\), from \\(98\\) down to \\(2\\). Every valid pair must arise this way, and different values of \\(y\\) give different ordered pairs. Therefore there are \\(49\\) pairs, choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
